'''Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses, S.A.''' (commonly known as '''NTE''') is a Balboan {{w|data center}} infrastructure company headquartered in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], [[Sant Cristòfor]]. Founded in 2024 by former [[Abyala Telecom]] executive [[Marc Tubau i Ferrer]] and electrical engineer [[Laura Pons i Vives]], the company builds and manages data center facilities across [[Balboa]] and southern [[Abyala]]. NTE is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker NTE and has signed infrastructure contracts with [[Kizan Systems]] of [[Okaiken]] to host the company's {{w|tensor processing unit}} clusters outside the Sanese archipelago, and with [[Sistemes OrSolar]] of Sant Cristòfor to host compute infrastructure for the [[XatXat]] large language model.

The company has attracted controversy over its energy consumption, its funding of a new {{w|natural gas}}-fired power station in [[Portblanc]], and indigenous protests against a proposed facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] in the [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] highlands.

== History ==

=== Founding (2024) ===

Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses was founded in early 2024 by Marc Tubau i Ferrer and Laura Pons i Vives. Tubau had spent over a decade at [[Abyala Telecom]], where he served as senior director of network infrastructure and oversaw the company's data centre operations across its six markets. In that role, he identified a structural gap in southern Abyala's digital infrastructure: while telecom carriers had invested heavily in mobile and broadband networks, the region lacked purpose-built data center capacity comparable to the facilities found in Okaiken, Volisania, or Breisland. Most enterprises in Balboa and neighbouring countries relied on colocation space in converted telecom rooms or overseas providers, with limited local options for high-density compute hosting.

Pons brought a background in electrical engineering and power systems design. She had previously worked on industrial power distribution projects in [[Estret Province|Estret]] and later specialised in cooling and power redundancy systems for critical facilities. The two founders combined complementary expertise: Tubau's understanding of the regional telecom and enterprise market with Pons's technical knowledge of data center mechanical and electrical systems.

The company was established as a public limited company ({{lang|ca|Societat Anònima}}, S.A.) from inception, with the founders intending to access public capital markets to fund the capital-intensive business of data center construction. Initial seed capital of approximately $15 million was provided by the founders and a small group of early investors, including former Abyala Telecom colleagues and Balboan private investors.

=== Kizan Systems contract (2024) ===

Later in 2024, NTE secured a multi-year infrastructure contract with [[Kizan Systems]], the Okaiken-based semiconductor and artificial intelligence hardware company. Under the agreement, NTE would build and operate data center facilities in Balboa and Potocsí designed to house Kizan's {{w|tensor processing unit}} clusters, providing the Okaiken company with compute infrastructure outside its domestic market. The contract was significant for both parties: Kizan Systems gained a foothold in southern Abyala for its international cloud and AI compute services, while NTE secured an anchor tenant that validated its business model and provided long-term revenue certainty.

The Kizan contract was the catalyst for NTE's accelerated path to public markets. The capital required to construct purpose-built data center facilities — including power infrastructure, cooling systems, and security — exceeded what the company could raise through private channels alone. The founders determined that an initial public offering would provide the most efficient route to funding the construction programme.

=== Initial public offering (2025) ===

NTE completed its initial public offering on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in mid-2025, raising approximately $120 million through the sale of new shares. The IPO valued the company at approximately $800 million at the offering price. The proceeds were allocated primarily to land acquisition and construction of the company's first data center facilities — one near the [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]] in [[Jala Province|Jala]], and one in [[Portblanc]], [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] — as well as to the procurement of power and cooling infrastructure.

The offering was noted for the speed of the company's path to market, approximately 18 months after founding. The Kizan Systems contract was cited in the IPO prospectus as a material factor in the company's investment case, providing contracted revenue that supported the valuation. The stock began trading under the ticker NTE and was well-received by investors seeking exposure to digital infrastructure in the Abyalan growth market.

=== Sistemes OrSolar contract (2025) ===

In 2025, NTE signed a compute hosting contract with [[Sistemes OrSolar]], the Sant Cristòfor-based artificial intelligence company developing the [[XatXat]] large language model. Under the agreement, NTE would provide data center capacity for OrSolar's model training and inference workloads, hosting the computing infrastructure used to develop and run the XatXat system. The contract represented NTE's second major anchor tenant after Kizan Systems and established the company as a infrastructure provider for both international and domestic AI compute workloads in Balboa.

The OrSolar contract was notable for the proximity of the two companies: both are headquartered in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], Sant Cristòfor, with OrSolar based at the Plastic Beach research cluster and NTE's offices located in the same district. The arrangement allowed OrSolar to access purpose-built data center capacity without constructing its own facilities, while providing NTE with a domestic anchor tenant complementing the Kizan Systems contract.

=== Facility construction and expansion (2025–2026) ===

Following the IPO, NTE commenced construction of its first two facilities in Balboa. The Sant Feliu Data Center, located near the [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]] in [[Jala Province|Jala]], was the first to become operational, entering service in early 2026. The site was selected for its proximity to the nuclear plant's high-capacity power infrastructure, providing reliable baseload electricity for the facility's Kizan TPU clusters. The Portblanc Data Center, in the capital of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], entered service in mid-2026.

In 2026, NTE announced plans for additional facilities in [[Tapuya]] and [[Potocsí]], targeting growing enterprise and government demand for domestic data hosting in those markets. The company also announced a proposed facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] in the Tramuntana highlands, which drew immediate opposition from indigenous communities and leftist political parties (see [[#Controversy|Controversy]]).

