The Sant Feliu facility is the company's primary site, housing the main Kizan Systems TPU cluster. The site was selected for its proximity to the [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu]], Balboa's only nuclear power station, which provides stable baseload power. The Portblanc facility serves the Tramuntana highland region and is co-located with a new gas-fired power station funded by NTE (see [[#Portblanc gas power station|Portblanc gas power station]]).

NTE's business model centres on providing colocation and managed hosting services to enterprise and government clients, with [[Kizan Systems]] and [[Sistemes OrSolar]] as its anchor tenants. The company offers tiered service levels ranging from basic colocation — power, cooling, and physical security — to fully managed infrastructure including network connectivity, hardware maintenance, and monitoring.

== Major contracts ==

=== Kizan Systems ===

The infrastructure contract with [[Kizan Systems]] is NTE's most significant commercial relationship. Under the multi-year agreement, NTE builds and operates facilities designed to Kizan's specifications for housing its {{w|tensor processing unit}} clusters, which are used for {{w|deep learning}} training and {{w|inference}} workloads. Kizan Systems, which is based in [[Okaiken]] and subsidised by the Sanese government, had previously concentrated its compute infrastructure within the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] on the sub-arctic southern island of [[Yagikoshu]]. The partnership with NTE represented Kizan's first major expansion of its physical compute footprint outside the Sanese archipelago.

The arrangement allows Kizan Systems to offer low-latency AI compute services to clients in southern Abyala without routing workloads to Okaiken, reducing network latency and addressing data sovereignty considerations. For NTE, the Kizan contract provides a long-term anchor tenant that underpins the financial case for each facility, with the Okaiken company committing to capacity leases spanning multiple years.

=== Sistemes OrSolar ===

NTE's compute hosting contract with [[Sistemes OrSolar]], signed in 2025, provides data center capacity for the development and operation of the [[XatXat]] large language model. OrSolar, founded in 2023 by former Kizan Systems researchers [[Abe Zayna]] and [[Ryuko Vidal]], raised approximately $100 million in funding to develop XatXat but did not construct its own data center facilities, instead contracting NTE for managed hosting of its training and inference compute infrastructure.

The contract gives OrSolar access to purpose-built data center capacity at NTE's Sant Feliu facility, where the XatXat model was trained and where inference workloads are hosted. The arrangement allows OrSolar to scale its compute resources in line with model development without the capital expenditure of constructing and operating its own facilities. Both companies are headquartered in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], Sant Cristòfor, facilitating coordination between OrSolar's research team and NTE's operations staff.

== Controversy ==

=== Energy consumption and capacity concerns ===

NTE's rapid expansion has drawn criticism from the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]], which has raised concerns about the company's energy consumption and its impact on Balboa's electricity grid. Balboa's total electricity capacity is approximately 11,200 MW, and the addition of large-scale data center loads — each facility consuming several megawatts of power — has prompted questions about whether the national grid can absorb the additional demand without straining capacity, particularly during dry seasons when hydropower output declines.

The Socialist Front has argued that NTE's datacenters represent a new category of industrial consumption that was not anticipated in the government's energy planning, and that the company's rapid buildout is outpacing the country's ability to expand generation capacity. Critics have noted that while the Sant Feliu facility benefits from proximity to nuclear baseload power, the Portblanc facility required the construction of a new gas-fired power station, increasing Balboa's reliance on fossil fuels at a time when the government has committed to expanding nuclear and renewable capacity under the [[1996 Adonian Community Edict on a Green Transition for the New Millennium]].

=== Portblanc gas power station ===

To supply the Portblanc Data Center, NTE funded the construction of a new {{w|natural gas}}-fired power station in [[Portblanc]], operated by [[Energia Tèrmica de l'Estat de Balboa|ETEB]], the state-owned thermal generation company. The arrangement — under which NTE provided capital for the plant's construction in exchange for priority electricity allocation — was the first instance of a private company funding new generation infrastructure in Balboa, where all electricity generation has historically been managed by state-owned enterprises.

The deal drew criticism from the Socialist Front and environmental groups, who argued that a private technology company was effectively setting energy policy by funding fossil fuel infrastructure that the state had not planned for. Critics contended that the arrangement undermined the green transition by locking in additional gas-fired generation capacity for the lifetime of the plant, and that it created a precedent for private actors to influence the composition of Balboa's energy mix. NTE and ETEB defended the arrangement as a pragmatic solution to meet the datacenter's power requirements without drawing on the existing grid, and noted that the plant's output would also serve the broader Tramuntana region.

=== Sant Jorge indigenous protests ===

NTE's proposed facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]], in the far north of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], has faced sustained opposition from indigenous communities in the highland area. The proposed site is located on lands traditionally used by highland indigenous groups, who have raised concerns about land use, environmental impact, and the disruption of cultural sites by the construction and operation of a large-scale data center.

The protests have drawn attention to the broader tension between Balboa's push for digital infrastructure development and the rights of indigenous communities in the Tramuntana highlands, a region that has previously seen conflicts over hydroelectric dam construction and its impact on indigenous lands. As of August 2026, the Sant Jorge facility remained in the planning phase, with no construction permits issued.

